<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Don (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tanais, River Don (Russia)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Don is a river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 1,870 kilometres long, flowing from south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a>. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as the Tanais, it was regarded in antiquity as the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and the Sarmatians. Later it was the heartland of the Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>–Don Canal now links it to the Volga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/don-river.jpg" alt="Don (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Don_(Voronezh_Oblast).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Don is the fifth-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It rises in the town of Novomoskovsk, 60 kilometres south-east of Tula, and flows 1,870 kilometres to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov">Sea of Azov</a>. Its upper half meanders gently south, while the lower half makes a great eastern curve past Voronezh before the final stretch, an estuary, runs west-south-west. Its basin lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> basin to the west, the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> basin to the east and the Oka basin to the north. The main tributary is the Seversky Donets, and the main city on the river is Rostov-on-Don.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name derives from the Avestan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> word dānu. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, the Volga-Don region was the homeland of the Proto-Indo-Europeans around 4,000 BCE. In antiquity some Greek geographers viewed the river as the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it the Tanaïs, which was also the name of a city on it, situated in the Maeotian marshes; they also called it Iazartes, and Pliny gives its Scythian name as Silys. A Greek source traditionally, though not certainly, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a> of Greek mythology on the Don. Byzantine traders used the river, and it has been a major trading route ever since.</p><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> controlled much of the lower Don and built the limestone fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarkel">Sarkel</a> on its left bank to defend a vital portage between the Don and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. After the Mongol conquests most of the basin passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, which set up the trade outpost of Azak in the estuary, an area speculated to be the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> in the mid-14th century. The Don Cossacks, who settled the valley in the 16th and 17th centuries, took their name from the river. Its middle and upper reaches were not mapped accurately before the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gradually conquered the area in the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Volga-Don Canal, 101 kilometres long, links the Don with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> where the two rivers come within about 100 kilometres of each other, and the Tsimlyansk Dam raises the Don's level there, forming the Tsimlyansk Reservoir. Below it, three dam-and-ship-lock complexes maintain depth for 130 kilometres. The Kochetovsky Lock was built in 1914–19 and doubled in 2004–08. The region is the setting of Mikhail Sholokhov's novel And Quiet Flows the Don.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Don (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their forerunners were probably the Sauromatae, whom Herodotus in the fifth century BCE placed east of the <b>River Don</b> (Tanais) and described as descendants of Scythian youths and Amazons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo divides them into a western group on the lower <b>Don</b> and the 'Upper Aorsi' further east, who ruled most of the Caspian shore.</blockquote><a href="/state/aorsi">Aorsi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kutrigurs are named by Procopius and Agathias in the 6th century as a Hunnic or Bulgar people of the steppe between the <b>Don</b> and the Dnieper, kin to the Utigurs to their east.</blockquote><a href="/state/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a></li><li><blockquote>They settled in the steppe between the Kuban, the Sea of Azov and the lower <b>Don</b>, in contact with the Caucasus and Byzantine Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roxolani were one of the main Sarmatian tribes, first recorded between the <b>Don</b> and the Dnieper in the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/roxolani">Roxolani</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus places the Sauromatae east of the <b>Don</b>, beyond the Royal Scythians, in the 5th century BCE, and tells the legend that they descended from Scythian men and Amazons.</blockquote><a href="/state/sauromatae">Sauromatae</a></li></ul></section>
